 The dependency rule (ADR-0010): implementations and consumers depend on `traits`, never on each other's concretes. Only `server` knows the concrete backends, which is what makes "swap redb for FoundationDB" or "in-memory for etcd" a composition change rather than a refactor. The seam earned its keep in M4: swapping the production backend from TiKV to FoundationDB (ADR-0042) touched the `server` wiring and added a crate, and changed no consumer.
+
+---
+
+## 5.4 Blackbox validation — `wyrd-validate`
+
+The last row above is the one component that is deliberately **not** part of the running system. `wyrd-validate` (`crates/validate`, proposal 0017) answers "is this deployment correct?" from *outside*, the way any S3 client does: over the wire, against a running endpoint, with no privileged view of Wyrd's internals. It is pointed at a deployment — an operator's own cluster, or a loopback stack in CI.
+
+**Why a separate binary rather than a `wyrd` subcommand.** A subcommand would share the roles' dependency closure, and a validator that links Wyrd's own crates is testing Wyrd's types against Wyrd's types — its verdict would be self-referential. The property is therefore mechanical rather than a promise in a doc comment: `cargo xtask ci` runs a dependency-closure guard (`xtask::repo_guard`, registered in `HYGIENE_GUARDS` beside the stray-gitlink and `forbid(unsafe_code)` scans) that fails the build if the **normal** dependency closure of `wyrd-validate` — transitively, resolved under every feature, optional edges included — contains any `wyrd-*` crate. *Normal*, not total: dev-dependencies are unconstrained, because what a test harness links never ships in the binary.
+
+**What exists today.** The crate, its argument surface, and credential resolution: it parses, resolves, echoes its configuration and exits. It makes no S3 call yet — the client, scenarios, oracle and verdict arrive in later slices, and the flags below are the contract they fill in.
+
+| Flag | Value | Meaning |
+|------|-------|---------|
+| `--endpoint` | URL | the S3 endpoint under validation |
+| `--region` | region name | the region sent with every request |
+| `--bucket` | bucket name | the bucket the run operates in |
+| `--scenario` | scenario name | which declared scenario shape to run |
+| `--duration` | duration | how long the run lasts |
+| `--workers` | count | how many concurrent workers drive it |
+| `--seed` | seed | seeds the deterministic payloads and keys, so a run is reproducible |
+| `--out` | path | where the run's artifacts are written |
+| `--run-id` | identifier | names this run in its artifacts |
+| `--driver-placement` | `internal` / `external` | where the driver ran — recorded with the numbers, because a latency figure quoted without its placement is eventually quoted wrongly (proposal 0017 §10) |
+
+All ten are required and each takes a value. The parser is deliberately **stricter than `wyrd`'s own**: an unrecognised flag, a repeated flag, an empty value, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused with a message naming it, rather than absorbed. `wyrd`'s parser accepts an unknown `--flag` silently, and changing that would break existing invocations; this binary is new, so it grandfathers nothing — and a mistyped `--duration` that is quietly ignored (in either token position) misreports the run the tool claims to have measured.
+
+**Credentials come from the environment, never from a flag** — so they stay out of the process table: `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` + `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` first (what every S3 client and CI runner already sets), falling back to the gateway's own `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY` + `WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, so a developer already running a loopback gateway needs no second export. When both pairs are present, AWS wins. Both names of a pair must be set together: half a pair is an error naming the missing half, not a silent fall-through to the other identity. The echoed configuration prints the resolved access-key **id** and which pair it came from, and never the secret.
